{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target identifies latent heat / evapotranspiration as the single mechanism explaining cross-city variation, claiming arid cities have a LARGER nocturnal UHI because the urban-rural latent heat contrast is maximized, while humid cities have a smaller gap. But in arid regions the surrounding countryside has little moisture and therefore little evapotranspirative cooling, so the rural side also cannot cool as effectively at night — reducing, not maximizing, the urban-rural temperature gap. Empirically, nocturnal UHI is often most pronounced in humid climates with dense vegetation contrast (e.g., Atlanta vs. humid Southeastern countryside), because the rural countryside cools efficiently via evapotranspiration while impervious city surfaces cannot. The directionality asserted in the mechanism is therefore backwards.","counterexample":["Nocturnal UHI in humid, vegetated cities like Atlanta or São Paulo can exceed that of arid cities like Phoenix, contradicting the claim that aridity maximizes the urban-rural latent heat contrast and hence the night-time gap."],"premise":"If the rural countryside in an arid region has very little moisture, it cannot sustain much evapotranspirative cooling, so there is little latent-heat advantage for the rural side and the urban-rural nighttime gap should be small, not large.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"cities in arid regions exhibit a larger nocturnal gap because the urban-rural contrast in latent heat flux is maximized, whereas in humid regions both city and countryside have moisture and the latent heat difference is smaller"}],"target":"d023746a902513155a4a99b9b3a099e66751ce1833c0841db0fff840de8a804d"}]}